#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# airy_bi_zero

#% text
# `airy_bi_zero(k)` gives the `k`-th zero of the Airy function `Bi` (all its real zeros are negative); `airy_bi_zero(k, x0)` gives the `k`-th zero below `x0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The zeros are counted by sign changes of the machine `airy_bi` from `min(x0, 0)` downwards and refined by Newton's method at the working precision.

#% item
# Exact arguments stay unevaluated; `n` gives the values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
n(airy_bi_zero(1))
#% output
#= -1.173713222709128

#% cell
n(airy_bi_zero(3))
#% output
#= -4.830737841662016

#% cell
n(airy_bi_zero(1), 25)
#% output
#= -1.17371322270912792491997996247

#% cell
n(airy_bi_zero(2, -500))
#% output
#= -500.23996579701316

#% cell
n([airy_bi_zero(1), airy_bi_zero(2), airy_bi_zero(3)])
#% output
#= [-1.173713222709128, -3.271093302836353, -4.830737841662016]

#% text
# Bi vanishes there:

#% cell
airy_bi(n(airy_bi_zero(2), 30))
#% output
#= ball(1.6873234417731333878e-35, 9.17e-34)

